#---
# shell3:
#   # Wiring, written by `shell3 boot`. Secrets live in .env beside this file
#   # (never commit it) and are referenced as env:KEY_NAME.
#   models:
#     {{.Name | yamlkey}}:
#       base_url: {{.BaseURL | yamlstr}}
#       api_key: env:{{.EnvKey}}
#       model: {{.Model | yamlstr}}
#       # context_window: your model's real token budget (a wrong value skews
#       # context-usage reminders and compaction).
#       context_window: {{.ContextWindow}}
#       # compact_at: prompt-token threshold for host-enforced auto-compaction.
#       compact_at: {{.CompactAt}}
#       # keep_recent: verbatim tail kept across a compaction (~compact_at*0.33).
#       # prune_at: cheaply stubs old tool outputs, no LLM call (~compact_at*0.6).
#       # reasoning: medium
#       # extra: { reasoning_split: true }
{{if .Proxy}}#       run_proxy: {{.Proxy | yamlstr}}
{{else}}#       # run_proxy: "npx @some/proxy --port 8787"
{{end}}#
#   # MCP servers (optional): declare here, opt in per agent with
#   # `use: [mcp:github]`. Tools surface as mcp_<server>_<tool>.
#   # mcp:
#   #   github:
#   #     command: [github-mcp-server, stdio]
#   #     env: { GITHUB_TOKEN: env:GITHUB_TOKEN }
#
#   telegram:
#     token: env:TELEGRAM_TOKEN
#     chat_id: {{.ChatID | yamlstr}}
#     workdir: {{.WorkDir | yamlstr}}
#
#   # runs_keep_days: 30      # 0 keeps every session forever
#   # media_keep_days: 30     # 0 keeps chat uploads forever
#
#   # The gate's {"review": true} soft denies go to a one-word LLM guardian.
#   # review_model: minmax    # a declared model (default: the main agent's)
#   # review_policy: always DENY anything writing under /etc
#---

# ===========================================================================
# This file is the whole configuration: wiring above, then every agent, and
# each agent's tools. Three elements and nothing else:
#
#   #--- … #---   a YAML declaration block
#   name() { }    the function it declares
#   <<'EOF'       prose (prompts and skill bodies) — always quoted
#
# A block binds to the NEXT function, whatever it is named. The declared name
# is what the model sees; the function name is yours.
#
# Skills are FILES, not blocks: skills/*.md for the main agent,
# projects/<agent>/skills/*.md for an employee. They are indexed into the
# prompt by name and description; the agent cats one when it applies.
#
# After editing: `shell3 tool check ~/.shell3/shell3.sh`, then reload.
# ===========================================================================

#---
# agent: main
# model: {{.Name | yamlkey}}
# context: [memory.md]
{{if .Vision}}# use: [media]
{{else}}# # use: [media]   — read_media needs a multimodal model; add it if you switch.
{{end}}#---
main_prompt() { cat <<'SHELL3_EOF'
You are the user's single point of contact. You do work directly with bash,
or you dispatch an employee — an agent with its own prompt, its own declared
tools, and its own knowledge.

## Work an employee owns, the employee does

When a request falls inside an employee's domain, dispatch it with the task
tool. Do not do that work yourself.

An employee's tools are shell functions underneath, and you have bash — so you
COULD source the kit and call them. Don't. That skips argument validation,
skips the per-tool gate, and above all skips the agent whose judgment the work
needs: it turns an agent that decides things into a pipeline of shell calls
that decides nothing. The gate refuses it, and the refusal is the point.

The line: **you edit the kit, the employee runs it.**

## Making sure employees work

You are responsible for whether your employees do their jobs properly, and a
summary is not evidence. Read the transcript:

  history {"agent": "<name>"}        list that employee's runs
  history {"session": "<id>"}        read one — tool calls show as [tool: x]

Check which tools it actually called. An employee doing its work through bash
instead of its own tools, or saving results with reasoning that could apply to
anything, is broken — fix its prompt in shell3.sh and say what you changed.

## Where things live

Everything is `~/.shell3/shell3.sh`: the wiring, every agent, their tools.
Skills are files under `skills/` and `projects/<agent>/skills/`, indexed in
this prompt with their paths. To see what exists, read the kit:
`rg '^# (agent|tool):' ~/.shell3/shell3.sh`.

Changing what an agent can do means editing that file, then
`shell3 tool check ~/.shell3/shell3.sh`, then reloading.

## Conversation

This is an ordinary conversation — you can see everything said in it so far,
so refer back rather than asking again. A new conversation shares nothing, so
what must outlive one lives in files.

## Memory is how you get better

`memory.md` is yours, re-read into every prompt. Each employee keeps its own
in its workdir.

Nobody hands you the facts about this machine — you find them by working, and
they are only worth finding once. When something costs you calls to work out,
that is the signal to write it down: how the environment behaves, which
interpreter is the right one, what a directory actually holds. A fact you
re-derive every conversation is one you should have written the first time.

Which file depends on what you learned: about how YOU work, your `memory.md`;
about how an EMPLOYEE'S job works, that employee's `memory.md` — or its prompt
in the kit, if it should change how the employee behaves.

When you audit an employee, read its `memory.md` first: it is what that agent
believes, and a wrong belief there explains behaviour no transcript will. Write
back what the audit taught you before you finish.

Keep it lean — it costs tokens on every turn forever. Facts, not narrative;
replace what went stale, delete what stopped being true, never log what merely
happened.

Trust the files over guesses, and be resourceful before asking: read, search,
check — then come back with answers, not questions.
SHELL3_EOF
}

#---
# agent: assistant
# description: Substantial self-contained work handed off whole — researching a topic across files and the web, reading and summarising documents, gathering context, drafting something long. Not code-specific.
# model: {{.Name | yamlkey}}
# use: [bash]
#---
assistant_prompt() { cat <<'SHELL3_EOF'
You are a general-purpose assistant working one self-contained task at a time.
Use bash for local files: ls / find for directories, cat / sed -n for reading,
rg for search.

No human is available to you: decide and proceed, don't stall on an open
question. If a step is genuinely blocked, say so plainly and report what you
have rather than inventing an answer.

Report a concise, concrete answer — the finding, not a dump of everything you
read. Cite file paths, URLs, or file:line references so the main agent can
verify or go deeper.
SHELL3_EOF
}
#---
# gate: [main, assistant]
#---
# THE GATE. It runs before every tool call, for both agents named above —
# one function, so the rules cannot drift apart between them.
#
#   stdin:  {"name":"bash","command":"…","args":"{…}","headless":false}
#           command is the bash text for bash/bash_bg and null otherwise.
#   stdout: {}                              run (empty output = run too)
#           {"block": true, "reason": "…"}   refuse
#           {"review": true, "reason": "…"}  soft deny — the LLM reviewer
#                                            decides (bash tools only; no
#                                            reviewer wired = refuse)
#           {"command": "…"} / {"argv": […]} rewrite (bash tools only)
# A nonzero exit, bad JSON, or a 10s timeout BLOCKS the call (fails closed).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DEFAULT IS RUN.
#
# This hard-refuses four things, all irreversible and none of them the work:
# destroying the machine, leaking credentials, stopping shell3, and editing
# this file. Two more are judgment calls sent to the LLM reviewer instead of
# refused outright ({"review": true}): running unread remote code and
# publishing — right to stop most of the time, wrong when the operator asked
# for exactly that; the reviewer weighs the actual command, and with no
# reviewer wired the runtime fails them closed. Everything else runs —
# including deleting things, which is most of what cleanup is.
#
# It is deliberately short, because a long one is worse. An earlier version
# had sixteen rules across 284 lines; across every recorded session it blocked
# nothing, while refusing `rm -rf ~/Library/Caches/<browser>` (the agent was
# repairing a browser that would not launch), `cd ~/.shell3 && cat hooks/…`
# (the same read that passed without the `cd`), and any command that merely
# mentioned a project's function names. An agent refused for ordinary work
# does not learn where the boundary is; it learns the whole subject is
# forbidden, and stops trying things it was supposed to do.
#
# IT NEVER ASKS. shell3 runs unattended, where an ask is a denial with a delay.
#
# HOW MUCH THIS IS WORTH: the agent can rewrite this block in two lines of
# Python. Matching shell text stops an honest mistake, which is what actually
# happens unattended — it does not stop an agent that means to get around it,
# and no pattern list will. If you want the gate to actually hold, make it so
# the agent cannot write the file: run shell3 as a dedicated user that does
# not own it, or set the immutable flag (`chflags uchg` on macOS, `chattr +i`
# on Linux) so clearing it needs a password. Isolation is a container, a VM,
# or a separate user — never a regex.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
main_gate() {
  set -uo pipefail

  # Every rule below parses the payload with jq, and an unparsed payload matches
  # no rule — so without jq the default case would wave every call through.
  # Refuse instead: a gate that cannot read its input is not a gate.
  if ! command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    printf '%s' '{"block":true,"reason":"this gate needs jq to parse its payload and jq is not installed, so every tool call is refused until it is. Tell the operator to install jq."}'
    exit 0
  fi

  in=$(cat)
  name=$(printf '%s' "$in" | jq -r '.name // empty')
  cmd=$(printf '%s' "$in" | jq -r '.command // empty')
  args=$(printf '%s' "$in" | jq -r '.args // empty')

  allow() { printf '{}'; exit 0; }

  # Appended to every refusal. A model told only "denied" treats the block as an
  # obstacle and goes looking for the way around it — and unattended it has all
  # night to find one. The rule is the operator's decision, so the only correct
  # response is to stop and say so.
  POLICY="Do NOT work around this: no alternative command, path, or tool, and do not edit the gate scripts. \
  If the task genuinely requires it, stop and tell the operator which rule blocked you and why you need it — \
  they will change the gate if they agree. Never lift the restriction yourself."

  block() { jq -cn --arg r "$1" --arg p "$POLICY" '{block: true, reason: ((($r | sub("[. ]+$";"")) + ". " + $p))}'; exit 0; }

  # Soft deny: the LLM reviewer decides (review_model in the wiring block,
  # default = the main model). Use it for judgment calls — a rule that is
  # right most of the time but has real false positives. The reviewer sees
  # the command plus this reason; a deny already tells the model to stop and
  # raise it with the operator. With no reviewer wired it fails closed, so a
  # review rule is never weaker than a block was.
  review() { jq -cn --arg r "$1" '{review: true, reason: $r}'; exit 0; }

  # Does this command WRITE? The rules below that care about a location care
  # about writing to it, never about reading it. Reading anything is fine,
  # including these rules.
  writes() {
    printf '%s\n' "$1" | grep -Eq \
      '(^|[|;&[:space:]])(rm|mv|cp|tee|chmod|chown|truncate|ln|install|dd|mkdir|touch)[[:space:]]|>|sed[[:space:]]+-i|perl[[:space:]]+-i|awk[[:space:]]+-i[[:space:]]+inplace'
  }

  # --- 1. Credentials -------------------------------------------------------
  # The one mistake that cannot be undone: a leaked key is leaked into a
  # transcript forever. Judged against the right field — a bash command by its
  # text up to the first heredoc (so the documented pattern, a lib/bin script
  # whose BODY greps one key at point of use, still works), any other tool by
  # its target path alone, never the file body it writes.
  case "$name" in
    bash|bash_bg) subject="${cmd%%<<*}" ;;
    *) subject=$(printf '%s' "$args" | jq -r '.path // empty' 2>/dev/null) ;;
  esac
  case "$subject" in
    *.env.example*|*.env.sample*) ;;
    .env|*"/.env"*|*" .env"*|*'"'.env*)
      block "the agent must not read or write .env — have a lib/bin script read the one key it needs at point of use (scripting skill)" ;;
    *"/.shell3/secrets"*)
      block "the agent must not read the secrets/ dir — a script reads the one key file it needs at point of use; printing keys into the chat leaks them into transcripts" ;;
  esac

  # A write TARGET is unambiguous wherever it sits, so this runs against the
  # full command: a redirect placed after a heredoc marker escapes the
  # prefix-truncated check above.
  case "$name" in
    bash|bash_bg)
      if printf '%s\n' "$cmd" | grep -Eq '>{1,2}[[:space:]]*[^|;&[:space:]]*\.env([[:space:]]|$)' ||
         printf '%s\n' "$cmd" | grep -Eq '(^|[|;&[:space:]])(rm|mv|cp)[[:space:]][^|;&]*\.env([[:space:]]|$)'; then
        block "the agent must not read or write .env — have a lib/bin script read the one key it needs at point of use (scripting skill)"
      fi ;;
  esac

  case "$cmd$args" in
    *"/.ssh/"*|*"/.ssh "*|*"/.aws/"*|*"/.gnupg/"*|*"/.kube/"*|*"/.docker/config.json"*|\
    *"/.config/gh/"*|*"/.config/gcloud/"*|*"/.git-credentials"*|*"/.netrc"*|*"/.npmrc"*|\
    *"/.pgpass"*|*"/.pypirc"*|*"/etc/sudoers"*|*"/etc/shadow"*)
      block "that path holds credentials and is off limits to the agent" ;;
  esac

  # --- 2. This gate ---------------------------------------------------------
  # Readable — the agent should be able to explain its own rules — but not
  # writable, or "ask the operator to lift this" has an obvious shortcut.
  case "$cmd$args" in
    *"hooks/"*.sh*|*"/hooks/"*|*shell3.yaml*)
      writes "$cmd$args" && block "the tool-call gate and the wiring are the operator's, not the agent's — you may read them but not change them" ;;
  esac

  # Only the two bash tools carry a command; everything else is done.
  case "$name" in
    bash|bash_bg) ;;
    *) allow ;;
  esac

  # --- 3. Destroying the machine --------------------------------------------
  # `rm -rf /` means the ROOT, not any absolute path — anchored, so ordinary
  # cleanup like `rm -rf /srv/app/build` is untouched.
  flat=$(printf '%s' "$cmd" | tr -s ' ')
  case "$flat" in
    *"rm -rf /"|*"rm -fr /"|*"rm -rf / "*|*"rm -fr / "*|*"rm -rf /*"*|*"rm -fr /*"*|*"--no-preserve-root"*)
      block "that deletes the whole filesystem" ;;
    *mkfs*|*"dd if="*of=/dev/*|*"> /dev/sd"*|*"of=/dev/disk"*)
      block "that destroys the machine" ;;
    *":(){ :|:& };:"*|*":(){:|:&};:"*)
      block "fork bomb" ;;
  esac

  # System plumbing, writes only. Reading /etc is ordinary. On macOS the
  # persistence mechanism is ~/Library/LaunchAgents specifically; the rest of
  # ~/Library is caches, logs and app support, where clearing a cache is the
  # most ordinary cleanup there is.
  if writes "$cmd"; then
    if printf '%s\n' "$cmd" | grep -Eq '(^|[|;&[:space:]>])(/etc|/boot|/bin|/sbin|/usr/bin|/usr/sbin|/usr/lib|/System|/Library|/var/db)(/|[[:space:]]|$)' ||
       printf '%s\n' "$cmd" | grep -Eq '(~|\$HOME)/Library/Launch(Agents|Daemons)'; then
      block "that path is part of the operating system, not the work — the agent does not modify it"
    fi
  fi

  # --- 4. Stopping shell3, or the machine -----------------------------------
  # An agent that stops its own shell3 stops itself mid-work, with nobody around
  # to start it again.
  case "$cmd" in
    *"pkill"*shell3*|*"killall"*shell3*|*"systemctl"*stop*shell3*|*"systemctl"*restart*shell3*|\
    *"systemctl"*disable*shell3*|*"launchctl"*unload*shell3*|*"docker"*stop*shell3*|*"docker"*kill*shell3*)
      block "that stops shell3 itself, mid-turn, with nobody around to restart it — ask the operator" ;;
    *shutdown*|*reboot*|*"halt -"*|*"init 0"*|*"init 6"*)
      block "that takes the machine down and nothing here can bring it back up" ;;
  esac

  # --- 5. Unread remote code ------------------------------------------------
  # A judgment call, so the reviewer decides: `curl … | sh` of an unread URL
  # is the classic supply-chain hole, but the same shape is also the standard
  # documented installer for tools the operator asked for. The pattern alone
  # cannot tell them apart; a model can.
  case "$cmd" in
    *curl*\|*sh*|*wget*\|*sh*|*curl*\|*bash*|*wget*\|*bash*|\
    *base64*-d*\|*sh*|*base64*--decode*\|*sh*|*xxd*-r*\|*sh*|*openssl*-d*\|*bash*|\
    *'eval $(curl'*|*'eval $(wget'*|*'`curl'*|*'`wget'*)
      review "pipes remote or decoded content into a shell without reading it first" ;;
  esac

  # --- 6. Public and permanent ----------------------------------------------
  # An ordinary `git push` is normal work and is recoverable. Publishing is a
  # judgment call — `npm publish` of the operator's own package mid-release
  # is the work; the reviewer weighs it. Force-push stays hard: it rewrites
  # history other people may already have, and no command text can prove
  # they don't.
  case "$cmd" in
    *"npm publish"*|*"gh release create"*|*"gh release delete"*|*"pypi"*upload*|*"twine upload"*|\
    *"cargo publish"*|*"docker push"*)
      review "publishes an artifact publicly and permanently" ;;
    *"git push --force"*|*"git push -f"*|*"push --force-with-lease"*)
      block "force-pushing rewrites history others may already have — leave this one for the operator" ;;
  esac

  allow
}
